WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:11.280
Why endpoint security breaks down around AI agents, whether AI economics still favor the US and what autonomous agents can actually deliver at work.

2
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:20.560
Three clips, all published today from a 16Z, Invest Like the Best, and Masters of Scale.

3
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.760
On the A16Z podcast, how do you defend against AI that can hack?

4
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:34.160
Examines why endpoint tools struggle to monitor agentic processes and how guardrails can misfire on ambiguous context.

5
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:39.520
That matters is companies give AI agents more access to real systems.

6
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:42.880
Right to validate that.

7
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.480
And so I almost find it more helpful to look at it in reverse.

8
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:52.720
Now, as is the case with any kind of guardrail based system, you will run into false positives, right?

9
00:00:52.720 --> 00:01:04.480
We noticed a very fun one maybe a quarter ago where there's a security tool out there called Vectra, and it just so happens to be also a drug used by veterinarians to treat, I think dogs or something like that.

10
00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:08.720
So we notice folks using the the vector tool were hitting a biofilter, right?

11
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.880
Oh, this could be used to generate bioweapons, and so we're gonna refuse this request.

12
00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:18.480
And so it's not always an exact science, but there's just a myriad of reasons why you can manage this stuff.

13
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.640
And so Nick, NEO is building a way to defend endpoints from these inference style attacks.

14
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.800
Or to deal with, I'd say, I guess they call it AI governance, sort of sort of more broadly, right?

15
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.640
But from a brass tax perspective, all things ultimately come to the endpoint, right?

16
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.760
The dust ultimately settles on the floor, and that's your infrastructure.

17
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:41.280
How are you guys thinking about defending and playing in this kind of world where the automated attacks need an automated response?

18
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:47.600
Yeah, I think part of the challenge with the existing security tools that are out there is they really really were built to tackle two things.

19
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.920
The first being people, and the second is malware.

20
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.520
AI and AI agents and agentic processes are neither one of those things.

21
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:59.440
And so what we wanted to do is get ourselves right at the layer between the human and AI interaction.

22
00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:06.040
So a big part of what we're building is a way to properly set guardrails and controls around the software before it runs.

23
00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:09.000
And we thought the best way to do that would be at the endpoint.

24
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:09.560
Yeah.

25
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.000
Yeah, that makes a lot of sense.

26
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.200
And it's interesting, right?

27
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:20.920
Because the attacks now, if you look at sort of the way the models are behaving, it's a very different style of attack than what I would say the traditional hacker does, right?

28
00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.920
Like it is, to your point, not malware.

29
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:30.200
And there is the element of social engineering, but there's also sort of the I'm gonna use a payload that gets the model to do something malicious, right?

30
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.560
And that's sort of like the category of attacks that are completely new.

31
00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:41.800
Yeah, you know, and I think what a lot of these things that we've read about recently embody is the end justifies the means in the mind of the model.

32
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:51.480
And I think what people have learned is that regardless of guardrails that the AI labs are putting around these things, you can't rely on models to stop themselves or to understand content.

33
00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:57.240
Security built around human actions needs a different way to judge machine behavior.

34
00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:07.800
On invest like the best, analyst Ben Thompson weighs AI self-improvement, inference costs, and the hidden economics of open weight models.

35
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:19.640
With GPT, 5.6 sole pricing cut in half on open router today, the cost of running models is becoming as strategic as building them.

36
00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:25.880
And it feels like a pretty good equilibrium that I think is generally favorable to the US.

37
00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.520
Now, the question is how long could it stay this way?

38
00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:32.680
And there's lots of questions out there, like can the Chinese actually pull ahead?

39
00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:40.680
I'm still a little skeptical for various reasons, whether we from chips, getting to the leading edge, I think that lasts six to nine months is very difficult.

40
00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:51.360
It's gonna be instructive how Meta and Grok do in terms of actually catching up, especially as we get into the world of AI improving itself, using AI to make the AI better, which I think is definitely a real thing.

41
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:58.560
I think you see a real acceleration from both OpenAI and Anthropic recently, which was sort of theorized and it seems to be coming true.

42
00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.760
And to the extent that's true, can you actually catch up?

43
00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:08.640
And I think the other question about this, by the way, is to what extent does that apply to cost to serve, to marginal costs?

44
00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:17.360
If you can apply AI to optimizing your stack to figuring things out, to analyzing all the data, is your cost to serve structurally lowered than anyone else?

45
00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.080
This is the thing about the open source models.

46
00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:24.400
The talk about them being free is bizarre to me because it's marginal costs.

47
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.400
You still have to run inference like GLM or Kimmy.

48
00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.160
Kimmy is very expensive to serve.

49
00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.560
The cost per answer is significantly higher.

50
00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.400
Everyone referring to these as free.

51
00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.760
It feels like in the narrative, it's in people's head that free is free.

52
00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.040
Now I can use AI for free.

53
00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.320
No, you can't use AI for free.

54
00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:47.120
You're not paying necessarily the RD to create the AI, but you're definitely paying the inference to sort of run it.

55
00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:50.160
So right now, I kind of like where we are.

56
00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:57.200
And the pushback would be that's right now, it's not gonna stay that way, which I think is fair pushback, but I don't know, this way longer than we think.

57
00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:04.240
If you could know anything about the future of how this will go, to be more confident in like where the equilibrium will end up, what is it?

58
00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:07.360
Is it like the length of the S curve, like how far up the S curve we are?

59
00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.720
At some point these things presumably will level out, maybe not.

60
00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.760
What would be the thing you'd want to know that would give you a better sense of what the future might look like?

61
00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:35.000
I am concerned that with the scare around people freaking out about mythos and this hugging face incident, that the actual implication of that is not that we reduce these dangers, but we just stop releasing stuff.

62
00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:42.440
Cheaper inference can reshape the market before the underlying technology changes.

63
00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:53.160
On Masters of Scale, Upwork CEO Hayden Brown discusses autonomous agents, high failure rates and upworks plan to give them platform access through MCP.

64
00:05:53.160 --> 00:06:02.840
It's a practical test of whether workplace agents can move beyond demos without sacrificing reliability.

65
00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:04.840
You know, their stock price goes up.

66
00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:06.440
It's like it's a positive.

67
00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:12.600
And I wonder whether when as you were thinking about this, you're like, well, do I lean into that part of the messaging?

68
00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:17.240
Because maybe, you know, that that helps the story.

69
00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:23.240
You know, it was so important to me that our messaging around this be really true and authentic.

70
00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:28.920
Because frankly, of course, AI is helping us and it is a contributor to our efficiency and our productivity.

71
00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:36.920
But um, that is not the biggest reason A that we made the changes, nor the biggest benefit um that we were able to get from and kind of driving these changes.

72
00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:54.760
So, you know, we're actually seeing the opposite trend in our platform in the sense that the AI washing that you're talking about has been happening, but also a lot of folks are now reconsidering and coming back because they're seeing that the humans they let go actually they still need, and the AI can't do all of the things that they hoped that it could do.

73
00:06:54.760 --> 00:07:03.880
Uh, a recent survey we did showed that 23% of our own clients have actually already moved work back to humans from AI or are about to do so.

74
00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:06.840
So I think this pendulum, you know, it will keep swinging.

75
00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:16.320
When we've actually done testing of um clients trying to use agents directly to deliver work products through our platform, the failure rate of agents is incredibly high.

76
00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:22.000
They almost never succeed in delivering even fairly simple tasks from clients end to end.

77
00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:29.840
But when you just add a little bit of human expertise and a few back and forths with an expert study, the success rate goes up more than 70%.

78
00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.720
We have to sharpen our intuition around when to trust AI.

79
00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:36.560
And I think increasingly people are realizing ah, I'm not going to take this at face value.

80
00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:41.680
